{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And see</b> — if there is in my heart even one moment of thought regarding Your matters that is not proper, that it not be pleasing in Your eyes — put me to death immediately." "<b>The word עֹצֶב</b> — like מְרִי, as in וּמֵרוּ וְעִצְּבוּ אֶת רוּחַ קָדְשׁוֹ (Isaiah 63:10); they intend grief. And the meaning: that HaShem, in the way of a metaphor, is grieved over the matter. And know that the vav of וְנַחֲנִי is like the vav in וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁלִישִׁי (Genesis 22:4), וַיִּשָּׂא אַבְרָהָם אֶת עֵינָיו, and וַיַּעֲזֹב אֶת עֲבָדָיו — and whoever does not set his heart to the word of HaShem; and this is a raphe pe in the language of Ishmael, and a man has no ability to speak of it in the language of Edom." "<b>בְּדֶרֶךְ עוֹלָם</b> — it is like אֶל בֵּית עוֹלָמוֹ, and אֹרַח לֹא אָשׁוּב אָהֳלֶךָ (Hosea 12:10), and הָאֹרֵחַ עוֹלָם תִּשְׁמֹר — as I interpreted it in its place."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 139, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra's point about the vav appears to concern a grammatical-morphological analogy; the passage is compressed and the logic connecting the examples is not fully articulated. The phrase about 'the language of Ishmael' and 'the language of Edom' refers to Arabic and a European vernacular respectively; translated literally without expansion."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:42:27.212924808Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}